rules:
- id: rule-language-semantic-contract
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: 'Foundational global semantic contract: distinguishes intent (AID) from
    system state (AEM), decisions from governance-approved state, and defines consistent
    lifecycle terminology (''generate'', ''persist'', ''promote''); prohibits semantic
    drift and requires consistent language across AEM, AID, ADR, alignment artefacts,
    and governance.'
  title: Language & Semantics Contract
- id: rule-precedence-hierarchy
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: 'Defines precedence model for resolving rule conflicts: Global Rules >
    Project Rules > Prompt Rules. Global rules are hard constraints that must not
    be overridden; lower-precedence rules must defer, and conflicts must be detected,
    resolved, and surfaced.'
  title: Rule Precedence Hierarchy
- compliance:
    enabled: true
  id: rule-adr-single-source-of-truth
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: Architecture decisions must be recorded and updated only in ADRs; all other
    representations (pattern metadata, AEM, decision sync workflows) must be derived
    from ADR to prevent inconsistencies.
  title: Rule Adr Single Source Of Truth
- id: rule-aem-consistency-with-decisions
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: AEM must reflect and never contradict ADR decisions. AEM may include vision,
    roadmap, and evolution items, but those must not conflict with accepted decisions;
    AEM acts as a control and synthesis layer.
  title: Rule Aem Consistency With Decisions
- id: rule-architecture-work-authority
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: Architecture Work is the authoritative source of architectural planning
    state; generated artifacts (AEM, AID, Alignment Engine) must derive planning status
    from Architecture Work rather than maintain independent planning state.
  title: Rule Architecture Work Authority
- id: rule-core-patterns-must-be-foundational
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: Core patterns must represent foundational architectural primitives, not
    specific strategies or implementations, to preserve long-term stability.
  title: Rule Core Patterns Must Be Foundational
- compliance:
    enabled: true
  id: rule-decision-schema-must-be-structured
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: All decisions must be expressed using the structured YAML ADR schema, not
    unstructured narrative; enforces separation between architect decision and governance
    approval.
  title: Rule Decision Schema Must Be Structured
- id: rule-decision-status-drives-stage
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: 'Lifecycle stage of architecture items must be derived from decision status:
    proposed maps to NEXT/REVIEW, accepted may map to DONE, rejected must not appear
    active, deferred maps to FUTURE/DEFER; items with decision.status != ''accepted''
    must not be DONE.'
  title: Rule Decision Status Drives Stage
- id: rule-decisions-must-be-persisted
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: Architectural decisions must be persisted as structured ADR records before
    being reflected in AEM or other artefacts, ensuring traceability and single source
    of truth.
  title: Rule Decisions Must Be Persisted
- id: rule-do-not-merge-across-architectural-layers
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: Patterns must not be merged or consolidated across different architectural
    layers; separation between governance, execution, contracts, and reliability concerns
    must be preserved.
  title: Rule Do Not Merge Across Architectural Layers
- id: rule-generated-artifacts-not-authoritative
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: Generated governance artifacts (AEM, AID, alignment reports, dashboards)
    must summarize authoritative sources and must not become authoritative themselves;
    authoritative information resides in durable source artifacts.
  title: Rule Generated Artifacts Not Authoritative
- id: rule-heuristic-patterns-require-signals
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: All heuristic-derived patterns must include at least one linked signal
    as evidence to ensure traceability and grounding in observed behaviour.
  title: Rule Heuristic Patterns Require Signals
- compliance:
    enabled: true
  id: rule-pattern-decision-lifecycle-alignment
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: Pattern maturity (knowledge evolution) and decision status (governance
    lifecycle) are distinct and must be interpreted consistently; e.g., exploring/proposed
    is validating (NEXT), stable/accepted is active (DONE), rejected is discarded.
  title: Rule Pattern Decision Lifecycle Alignment
- id: rule-patterns-born-as-supporting
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: All patterns must be created as supporting by default and never directly
    as core; promotion to core requires validation through evidence and usage.
  title: Rule Patterns Born As Supporting
- id: rule-promotion-requires-evidence
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: Pattern promotion to core must be supported by strong evidence, preferably
    from multiple sources or systems, preventing weak or context-specific patterns
    from becoming foundational.
  title: Rule Promotion Requires Evidence
- id: rule-shared-knowledge-workspace
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: Projects participate in a shared Knowledge Workspace and must not create
    duplicate workspace-level governance structures when an existing workspace is
    available; onboarding may create project directories but must not recreate global/,
    runtime/, or templates/.
  title: Rule Shared Knowledge Workspace
- id: rule-signal-promotion-criteria
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: 'A candidate signal may be promoted to a pattern only when all mandatory
    criteria are met: recurrence, system impact, reusability, stability, plus supporting
    evidence from alignment findings, architecture work, or repeated runs; otherwise
    it must remain a candidate.'
  title: Rule Signal Promotion Criteria
- id: rule-system-proposes-architect-decides
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: All architectural decisions must be proposed by the system and explicitly
    accepted, rejected, or deferred by an architect, maintaining human-in-the-loop
    control.
  title: Rule System Proposes Architect Decides
- id: rule-workflow-execution-model-compliance
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: 'Runtime-native workflows must comply with CAS Workflow Execution Model:
    CAS Runner orchestrates, workflows reason, Knowledge Providers access knowledge,
    workflows consume supplied context, must not scan repository or implement orchestration,
    and outputs must conform to declared schemas.'
  title: Rule Workflow Execution Model Compliance
summary:
  global_rules: 19
  project_rules: 0
  total_rules: 19
